    "result_text": "The full suite passes: **66 tests run, 0 failures, 0 errors \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `capacityWarning` signal following the same persisted-field pattern the codebase already uses for `possibleDuplicate` (which, unlike the transient `bulkSignupWarning`, must survive the read-back GET the test performs).\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `capacityWarning` boolean field to the owner schema (the `OwnerDto` is generated from this at build time).\n2. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a persisted `Boolean capacityWarning` field (`@Column(name = \"capacity_warning\")`) with getter/setter.\n3. **Schema files** (hsqldb, h2, postgres, mysql) \u2014 added the `capacity_warning` column so the value survives a reload.\n4. **`AssignCapacityWarning.java`** \u2014 new OfficeFloor function that counts existing owners in the owner's city (case-insensitive, whitespace-collapsed \u2014 identical normalization to `EnsureCityCapacity`, so the warning boundary lines up exactly with the rejection) and sets `capacityWarning` true when the count is in `[40, 49]`. It runs before `save`, so the new owner isn't counted.\n5. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired `assignCapacityWarning` into the pipeline (after `assignPossibleDuplicate`, before `save`) under the transaction governance.\n\nThe `capacityWarning` reuses `EnsureCityCapacity`'s constant intent: 40\u201349 warns, and the hard rejection at 50 is untouched (verified by `Cp18Tests` still passing). Mapstruct auto-maps the new field by name, so no mapper change was needed.",
